Lymphoedema (Post-Mastectomy)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Localized Fluid Shift
Unilateral post-surgical arm swelling, Non-pitting edema with skin thickening, Sensation of limb heaviness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-028
Reason for Visit: Rapid worsening of unilateral arm swelling, skin tightness, and deep aching pain, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute cellulitis or deep vein thrombosis in a post-surgical limb.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute exacerbation of swelling in a post-mastectomy arm, accompanied by a feeling of extreme tightness and discomfort. This presentation mandated an urgent after-hours assessment to differentiate benign lymphoedema progression from an acute, limb-threatening bacterial cellulitis that could not safely wait for standard in-hours care.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Sudden heavy, swollen, and aching arm. Patient states, My right arm feels like a solid block of wood today; it is incredibly heavy, aching deeply, and my rings are completely stuck.
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known history of right-sided breast cancer treated with mastectomy and axillary node dissection five years ago. They report chronic, progressive, unilateral swelling of the ipsilateral arm. Over the last 48 hours, there has been a marked exacerbation. The patient describes an intense sensation of heaviness, fullness, and aching in the affected limb. They report the skin feels exceptionally tight and thick. There is discomfort and tightness of previously well-fitting clothing on the affected side. A restricted range of motion in the shoulder and upper extremity joints has developed rapidly due to the mechanical burden of the localized fluid shift. No systemic fevers or rigors are reported.
Functional Status: Functionally limited; the patient is unable to use the right arm for activities of daily living due to extreme heaviness and restricted motion.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of developing recurrent episodes of bacterial cellulitis; spouse is assisting with tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68.0 Kg
General: Patient is comfortable at rest but distressed by the impairment of the right arm, holding it supported on a pillow.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Strong radial pulse in the affected limb. Capillary refill is normal. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Integumentary/Extremities: The diagnostic triad is clinically confirmed. There is massive unilateral post-surgical arm swelling, with obvious asymmetry of wrist and arm circumference when compared to the contralateral limb. Examination reveals dense, firm, non-pitting edema due to subcutaneous fibrosis. The skin is tight, firm, and thickened, creating a positive Stemmer sign (inability to pinch a fold of skin at the base of the digits). Early hyperkeratosis and a peau d'orange texture are noted. Crucially, there is no localized heat, sharply demarcated erythema, or purulent weeping to suggest acute cellulitis.
Environmental Observations: Standard living environment; patient has access to elevation pillows.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has not been wearing their prescribed compression sleeve consistently.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lymphoedema (Post-Mastectomy) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of fever, localized warmth, sharply defined spreading erythema, and exquisite superficial tenderness, pointing to pure mechanical fluid overload.

Upper Extremity Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset of firm, fibrotic non-pitting edema rather than sudden, painful, cyanotic, and acutely pitting venous congestion.

Malignant Lymphatic Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, stable history of post-surgical swelling, though new-onset massive exacerbations require routine oncology follow-up to exclude local recurrence.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of secondary lymphoedema due to severe localized fluid shifts following surgical disruption of the axillary lymphatic pathways. The sudden mechanical burden of the protein-rich fluid has caused significant functional impairment and deep aching. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have caused significant, unrelieved distress and carried a risk of missing a rapidly progressing, silent cellulitis to which these limbs are highly susceptible. Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to definitively exclude acute infection, rule out deep venous occlusion, and instruct the patient on immediate mechanical decongestive strategies to relieve the acute tension. The expected investigation results include delayed radiotracer transit or dermal backflow on upper extremity lymphoscintigraphy that would assist in confirming the diagnosis formally if required.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassured patient regarding the absence of acute infection. Advised strict, continuous limb elevation above the level of the heart using supportive pillows. Instructed the patient to resume wearing the customized compression garment immediately upon reduction of the acute swelling.
Positive Behavioral Change: To consistently manage this swelling and prevent painful flare-ups, commit to a strict routine of elevating your arm above your heart for 45 minutes while watching evening television. Once the time is up, gently squeeze the arm and notice the slight reduction in tension. This physical relief and the regaining of flexibility will serve as the immediate reward for dedicating time to your mechanical drainage routine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized Lymphoedema Therapist for complex decongestive therapy and garment refitting.
DME/Safety (Optional): Supportive foam wedge for arm elevation.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in one week to assess volume reduction and review oncology surveillance schedule.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Seek immediate medical attention if the swollen arm suddenly becomes hot to the touch, develops rapidly spreading bright red patches, or if you develop a fever or severe chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This represents a mechanical failure of the lymphatic drainage system; management relies on consistent physical therapy rather than medication.
Clinical Course: Without the lymph nodes to drain fluid, protein-rich liquid pools in the arm, leading to firm swelling and thickening of the skin. Consistent compression, elevation, and specialized massage are required daily to force the fluid back into circulation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not allow anyone to take blood pressure readings, draw blood, or administer injections in the affected arm, as any minor skin break can lead to a severe, rapidly spreading bacterial infection.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The surgical removal of your lymph nodes removed the plumbing for this arm. Gravity and medical compression garments act as the artificial pump to move the fluid out of the tissues and back to your heart.
Actionable Steps: Elevate the arm continuously tonight above heart level. Resume wearing your compression sleeve daily. Avoid any skin trauma to the limb.

